# amp-emacs

Deep integration of Sourcegraph Amp with Emacs, inspired by amp.nvim.m

## Status: 🚧 Experimental / Prototype

This is a work-in-progress implementation. The protocol communication has been
reverse-engineered based on amp.nvim behavior and may need adjustments.

## Features

- ✅ Bidirectional communication with `amp --ide` via UNIX socket
- ✅ Automatic buffer notifications when switching files
- ✅ Cursor position tracking
- ✅ Text selection notifications
- ✅ User commands to send messages to Amp
- ⏳ Amp editing buffers directly (protocol TBD)
- ⏳ Diagnostics integration (flycheck/flymake)
- ⏳ Multi-project support

## Installation


### Prerequisites

1. Install Amp CLI:
   ```bash
   pnpm add -g @sourcegraph/amp
   # or
   npm install -g @sourcegraph/amp
   ```

2. Create an account at [ampcode.com](https://ampcode.com)

### Emacs Setup

#### For Doom Emacs

Add to your `packages.el`:

```elisp
(package! amp
  :recipe (:local-repo "~/path/to/amp-emacs"))
```

Add to your `config.el`:

```elisp
(use-package! amp
  :config
  (setq amp-auto-start t)
  (setq amp-server-log-level 'info))
```

#### For vanilla Emacs

Add to your load path and load it:

```elisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/path/to/amp-emacs")
(require 'amp)

(setq amp-auto-start t)
(setq amp-server-log-level 'info)

;; Optional: enable globally
(amp-mode 1)
```

#### For use-package users

```elisp
(use-package amp
  :load-path "~/path/to/amp-emacs"
  :config
  (setq amp-auto-start t)
  (setq amp-server-log-level 'info)
  :bind-keymap
  ("C-c a" . amp-mode-map))
```

## Usage

### Starting Amp

1. In Emacs:
   ```
   M-x amp-start
   ```

2. In your terminal (in your project directory):
   ```bash
   cd /path/to/your/project
   amp --ide
   ```

3. The terminal will show the Amp interface. Emacs will automatically notify
   Amp about:
   - Current file and cursor position
   - Text selections
   - Buffer changes

### Commands

| Command | Default Keybinding | Description |
|---------|-------------------|-------------|
| `amp-start` | `C-c a s` | Start Amp server |
| `amp-stop` | `C-c a q` | Stop Amp server |
| `amp-restart` | `C-c a r` | Restart Amp server |
| `amp-status` | `C-c a ?` | Show server status |
| `amp-send-message` | `C-c a m` | Send message to Amp |
| `amp-fix-region` | `C-c a f` | Ask Amp to fix selected code |
| `amp-improve-region` | `C-c a i` | Ask Amp to improve selected code |
| `amp-explain-region` | `C-c a e` | Ask Amp to explain selected code |
| `amp-prompt-for-region` | `C-c a p` | Send region with custom prompt |

### Example Workflow

1. Start Amp in Emacs: `M-x amp-start`
2. Open a terminal and run: `amp --ide`
3. In Emacs, open a file in your project
4. Select some code and press `C-c a f` to ask Amp to fix it
5. Or press `C-c a m` to send a custom message to Amp
6. Watch Amp respond in the terminal

## Configuration

### Basic Configuration

```elisp
;; Auto-start Amp server when Emacs starts
(setq amp-auto-start t)

;; Log level: 'debug, 'info, 'warn, or 'error
(setq amp-server-log-level 'info)

;; Throttle cursor notifications (seconds)
(setq amp-client-throttle-interval 0.1)

;; Custom data directory
(setq amp-server-data-home "~/.local/share/amp")
```

### Custom Keybindings

```elisp
;; Change the prefix key
(setq amp-keybindings-prefix "C-c A")

;; Or define your own
(with-eval-after-load 'amp
  (define-key amp-mode-map (kbd "C-c a a") #'amp-start)
  (define-key amp-mode-map (kbd "C-c a k") #'amp-stop))
```

## Architecture

The integration consists of three main components:

### 1. amp-server.el
- Manages UNIX socket server
- Handles lockfile coordination
- Processes JSON messages
- Provides message protocol

### 2. amp-client.el
- Sends buffer/cursor/selection notifications
- Hooks into Emacs events
- Provides user-facing message commands

### 3. amp.el
- Main entry point and user interface
- Minor mode definition
- Keybindings

## Protocol

Communication uses newline-delimited JSON over a UNIX socket:

### Messages from Emacs to Amp:

```json
{"type": "buffer_enter", "buffer": {"file": "/path/to/file.py", "line": 42, "column": 10}}
{"type": "cursor_moved", "buffer": {"file": "/path/to/file.py", "line": 43, "column": 5}}
{"type": "selection_changed", "selection": {"file": "/path/to/file.py", "start_line": 10, "end_line": 15, "text": "..."}}
{"type": "user_message", "message": "Fix this code"}
```

### Messages from Amp to Emacs:

```json
{"type": "ping"}
{"type": "diagnostics_request"}
{"type": "edit_request", "file": "/path/to/file.py", "edits": [...]}
```

## Troubleshooting

### Amp CLI doesn't connect

1. Check that the server is running:
   ```
   M-x amp-status
   ```

2. Check the lockfile exists:
   ```bash
   ls ~/.local/share/amp/ide/
   ```

3. Check logs in Emacs messages buffer: `M-x view-echo-area-messages`

4. Increase log level:
   ```elisp
   (setq amp-server-log-level 'debug)
   (amp-restart)
   ```

### Socket permission errors

Make sure the socket directory is writable:
```bash
mkdir -p ~/.local/share/amp/ide
chmod 755 ~/.local/share/amp/ide
```

## Development

### Testing Protocol Messages

You can test the protocol manually:

```bash
# In one terminal, start Emacs and run amp-start
emacs -Q -l amp.el -f amp-start

# In another terminal, connect to the socket
socat - UNIX-CONNECT:~/.local/share/amp/ide/emacs-XXXXX.sock

# Type JSON messages:
{"type":"ping"}
```

